BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Book Launch: Fighting for the American Dream
DTSTART:20260926T180000Z
DTEND:20260926T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260924T141115Z
UID:35bca24775938bb28c3f8e26b090d32dd78ffa73@corning-events
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:History & Heritage,Literature
DESCRIPTION:Book Launch: Fighting for the American Dream\nJoin us for the 
 book launch of Fighting for the American Dream: From Blue-Collar Kid to Ma
 verick Economist\, The Battle to Defend Working Families by Charles J. Wha
 len\, in conversation with Ian Greer!\nAbout the book: The book explores t
 he long erosion of the American Dream through the lens of a career memoir.
  It spotlights decades of rising worker insecurity\, including the irony o
 f how the author’s own career devoted to advancing the interests of stru
 ggling workers got caught in the same currents of precarity as the people 
 he aimed to defend. Combining practical insight into the American economy 
 since World War II with reflections on personal experience\, the book conn
 ects with a wide audience and promises to spark an overdue public discussi
 on that moves us toward a more prosperous\, sustainable\, and humane econo
 my for all.\nAbout the author: Charles J. Whalen is an author\, economist
 \, and research fellow at the Baldy Center for Law and Social Policy\, Uni
 versity at Buffalo. His career in economics spans four decades\, including
  work at the Congressional Budget Office\, BusinessWeek\, and Cornell Univ
 ersity. Whalen is a graduate of the Cornell University School of Industria
 l and Labor Relations and longtime resident of the Finger Lakes region. Fi
 ghting for the American Dream is his ninth book.\nAbout the conversation p
 artner: Ian Greer is director of the ILR Ithaca Co-Lab and a research prof
 essor at Cornell University School of Industrial and Labor Relations. He c
 arries out research and teaching in Ithaca and the surrounding region\, fo
 cusing on local and regional issues of concern to workers and their famili
 es\, such as living wages\, unemployment insurance\, and a "just transitio
 n" in manufacturing. He is co-author (with Charles Umney) of Marketization
 : How Capitalist Exchange Disciplines Workers and Subverts Democracy (Bloo
 msbury 2022). Greer\, an Ithaca resident\, holds a PhD in industrial and l
 abor relations from Cornell University.\n\nSource: FLXcalendar - https://c
 alendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78466282/20260926140000
GEO:42.441218;-76.498352
LOCATION:Buffalo Street Books\, 215 North Cayuga Street\, Ithaca\, New Yor
 k\, 14850\, United States
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.time.ly/o9b0uzpj/event/78466282/20260926140000
END:VEVENT
